WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.130
ROGER MARTINEZ: Does your
app deal with sensitive data?

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:03.880
Do you need to ensure
that it's protected?

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:04.920
Stay tuned.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.039
Find out how easily Cloud
DLP does this and much more.

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:16.550
Throughout this data
loss prevention series,

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:18.770
we're going to focus on our
fictitious feline walking

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:21.200
company, KittyCat Walks.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.990
KittyCat Walks pairs cat
owners with local cat walkers

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:26.930
via their online and mobile app.

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:28.820
KittyCat Walks launched
six months ago,

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:31.190
and it's rapidly
gaining customers.

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:33.420
They want to understand
more about their customers,

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:34.850
both human and feline alike.

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.160
And they need a way to share
and analyze some customer

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:41.820
information, like "purr-ferred,"
[CLEARS THROAT] preferred walk

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:45.530
length or favorite catnip, while
protecting sensitive data like

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:48.620
addresses and
credit card numbers.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:51.090
While this work would be
super hard for humans,

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:53.690
it's pretty easy for Cloud DLP.

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:55.910
Not only can Cloud DLP
automatically detect

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:57.650
addresses and
credit card numbers,

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:01.550
it has over 120
predefined info types.

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:03.860
You can even define your own
based on custom dictionary

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:05.790
detectors or
regular expressions,

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:08.610
so you can protect things
like customer account numbers.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.450
So what sort of
data can DLP scan?

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.720
Cloud DLP scans streams
of data, structured text,

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.910
files in cloud storage, or data
in BigQuery, and even images.

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:23.180
Once found and classified,
DLP can de-identify the data

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:27.140
using techniques like redaction,
masking, tokenization, date

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.592
shifting, and more.

00:01:29.592 --> 00:01:31.550
Using the right combination
of these techniques

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:33.110
allows you to preserve
the right data

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:35.180
and still retain some
referential integrity

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:38.780
to collect important metrics and
make smart business decisions.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:40.400
By adequately
de-identifying data

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.440
that we don't need for
any business purpose,

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.330
we can minimize the
likelihood of exposure

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:46.310
of sensitive customer
information, which

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:49.670
helps protect our
users and our business.

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:52.010
So what would happen
if we didn't adequately

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:53.910
de-identify our data?

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.000
Well, if that data fell
into the wrong hands,

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.100
we run the risk of exposing
sensitive information

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.620
about individuals, which can
have some serious privacy

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.210
implications.

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:05.660
Sounds scary, but Cloud DLP
can help compute and quantify

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:08.479
the risk according
to several metrics.

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:10.690
And it can publish its
findings and summary details

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:12.290
to the systems.

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:14.060
For our catwalk app,
it's clear that we're

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:16.400
collecting personal information
like credit card numbers

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:17.500
and addresses.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.630
But how do we use DLP to help
keep their customer data safe?

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:22.350
Stay tuned for the
next episode, where

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.830
we'll cover what is
data de-identification?

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:28.180
[MUSIC PLAYING]

